  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mr.Arunkumar vs The Food Safety Officer on 13 February, 2020 Author: Ashok Menon Bench: Ashok Menon In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr. Justice Ashok Menon Thursday, The 13Th Day Of February 2020 / 24Th Magha, 1941 Crl.MC.No.969 Of 2020(A) Against The Order/Judgment In St 2677/2015 Of Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Kalamassery,Ernakulam Petitioner/S: Mr.Arunkumar, Aged 37 Years S/O. Jayadevan, Jalajabhavan, Compayar P O, Kallar (Parathodu Village, Udumbanchola Taluk), Idukki District. By Advs. Sri.Ameer.K.M. Smt.M.J.Jesna Respondent/S: 1 The Food Safety Officer, Mobile Vigilance Squad,Ernakulam District, Pin-682306. 2 The State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam-682031. R1-2 By Addl. Director General Of Prosecution, Sri.Suresh Babu Thomas This Criminal Misc. Case Having Come Up For Admission On 13.02.2020",
    "facts": "The petitioner is the 1st accused in S.T.No.2677/2015 on the files of the Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Kalamassery for having allegedly committed offences punishable under Sections 3(1) (zx), 3(1) (zz), (v) (x) Section 26(2) (i) (ii), Section 51, Section 59 (i), 66 of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 (FSS Act 'for short'). The complaint was filed before the Judicial Magistrate on 17.11.2014.",
    "arguments": "The seizure of the food article was on 29.10.2013 and therefore the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submits that there is delay of more than one year as stipulated under 77 of the FSS Act and therefore the complaint ought to have been rejected at the threshold. It is also pertinent to note that the proviso to Section 77 specifically states that it is for the Commissioner of Food Safety to explain the reasons for the delay and grant further time. There is reference made in Annexure A4 to the report of the Director of CFL, Kolkotta which is dated 30.06.2014 and it is on the basis of that the food article was found to be in contravention with the provisions of the Act. The food article was sent for examination to the CFL at Kolkotta at the instance of the accused and without waiting for the report from there, no prosecution should have been launched. 2. The learned Additional Director General of Prosecution relies on the decision of State of Rajasthan v. Sanjay Kumar and others [1998 KHC 968] in support of his argument regarding the fact that under Section 469 Cr.P.C. also, the commencement of limitation of period can start only when the commission of offence was known to the person who is aggrieved by the offence or to the police officer. Under that provisions also the commission of offence could be said to have been revealed to the Food Safety Officer only on getting the report from the CFL, which is dated 30.06.2014 and within one year from that period, the complaint has been filed. The Honourable Supreme Court has in the above cited decision held as follows:- \"13.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "The seizure of the food article was on 29.10.2013 and therefore the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submits that there is delay of more than one year as stipulated under 77 of the FSS Act and therefore the complaint ought to have been rejected at the threshold. It is also pertinent to note that the proviso to Section 77 specifically states that it is for the Commissioner of Food Safety to explain the reasons for the delay and grant further time. There is reference made in Annexure A4 to the report of the Director of CFL, Kolkotta which is dated 30.06.2014 and it is on the basis of that the food article was found to be in contravention with the provisions of the Act. The food article was sent for examination to the CFL at Kolkotta at the instance of the accused and without waiting for the report from there, no prosecution should have been launched. 2. The learned Additional Director General of Prosecution relies on the decision of State of Rajasthan v. Sanjay Kumar and others [1998 KHC 968] in support of his argument regarding the fact that under Section 469 Cr.P.C. also, the commencement of limitation of period can start only when the commission of offence was known to the person who is aggrieved by the offence or to the police officer. Under that provisions also the commission of offence could be said to have been revealed to the Food Safety Officer only on getting the report from the CFL, which is dated 30.06.2014 and within one year from that period, the complaint has been filed.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "The Honourable Supreme Court has in the above cited decision held as follows:- \"13."
  },
